    <text>
        <body>
            <div xml:id="d502">

                <pb facs="0527" n="493" sameAs="frus:frus1897/pg493"/>

                <head><hi rendition="#italic">Mr. Sherman to Mr.
                            Taylor.</hi></head>
                <opener><dateline rendition="#float-right"><placeName><hi rendition="#small-caps"
                                >Department of State</hi>,<lb/><hi rendition="#italic"
                                >Washington</hi></placeName>, <date calendar="gregorian"
                            when="1897-06-25"><hi rendition="#italic">June 25,
                        1897</hi></date>.</dateline>
                    <seg rendition="#float-left">No. 714.]</seg></opener>
                <p><hi rendition="#small-caps">Sir</hi>: I inclose copy of a letter from the Hon.
                    Lemuel E. Quigg, transmitting one from Messrs. Hoadly, Lauterbach &amp; Johnson,
                    attorneys for Sartorius &amp; Co., of New York, who are the owners of some 719
                    bales of tobacco purchased or contracted for in the Island of Cuba prior to the
                    issue of the Governor-General’s prohibitory order of May 16, 1896.</p>
                <p>Permission to ship this tobacco has been refused, and you are instructed to exert
                    all proper pressure on the Spanish Government to have the desired permission
                    accorded.</p>
                <p>Respectfully, yours,</p>
                <closer><signed><hi rendition="#small-caps">John Sherman.</hi></signed></closer>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
